# Code

## Waiting

- Never `waitForTimeout`. Wait for a state: a web-first assertion, a locator, a URL (in a spec — a Page Object
  waits for what the screen shows, `ui.mdc`), or the answer the screen
  gives no other sign of (`whenAnswered`, `ui.mdc`).
- Never raise a timeout or retries as the only change.

## Checks

- Behaviour checks live in specs and assertion helpers. Page Objects and components expose locators, actions
  and readers and never check; API clients compose and send requests and never check.
- Never weaken an expected outcome to make a test pass; never change it unless the plan changed.
- Check real expected content from the contract or plan, not shape — a status code, `Array.isArray` or
  `toBeTruthy()` alone passes when the response is wrong in every way that matters:

  ```ts
  expect(Array.isArray(body)).toBe(true); // weak — any array passes
  const addresses = await expectJson(customerAddresses.list()); // strong — the expected content
  expect(addresses, "Addresses of the customer").toEqual(expect.arrayContaining([expect.objectContaining({ city: "Berlin", isDefault: expect.any(Boolean) })]));
  ```

  A structural check only when the plan has no known content yet (a brand-new account), saying so.
- Never a fake check (`expect(response).toBeDefined()`), never a `try/catch` around a check.
- Assert known values (the test's own data, a documented constant, a bound) — never re-implement the
  product's calculation; no defensive branches for what a check covers. Prove "created" by the test's own
  entity (its id, its `AQA` name), "nothing created" by no create request leaving the page — never by a
  shared list compared before and after.

## Locators

`getByTestId` → `getByRole` with its name → `getByLabel` → `getByPlaceholder` → `getByText` (stable) → CSS as
a last resort; only in Page Objects and components, never raw selectors in specs (`ui.mdc` §Locators).

## Readable code

A reviewer reads top to bottom without scrolling back: **every line acts, checks, or is needed to do one of
the two.** Deviate only when a case genuinely needs it, saying why in one comment. Guide sizes in statements
(a smell, not a quota): API test ~15–20, UI test ~25, a step ~8, a spec file ~250, a Page Object ~150.
- **Comments say why, briefly — never history**: one or two lines, only where code cannot say it (a product
  quirk, a workaround and its reason). No dates, "confirmed live", "used to", "fixed after" — history goes
  to the plan's change log and git; no comment restating the next line; a class or fixture header at most
  three lines. Findings (an odd locator, a missing test id, stand drift) go to the change log, not the code.
- **Names carry the meaning** (`disposableAddress`, `startCapturingCreateRequests()`, `expectSignedIn`) —
  no `data1`, `res2`, decoding abbreviations, never `helper`, `utils`, `data`, `manager`, `handleFlow`.
- **Nothing speculative or dead**: no method, type, option or helper "for later". An unused Page Object
  member fails the gate, an unused import, variable, parameter or fixture fails `tsc`, an unused export is
  warned — delete it. Code taken out of the suite is deleted (`01-workflow.mdc` §Cleanup).
- **Plain code**: a call or object fitting ~120 characters stays on one line, a longer one breaks once per
  argument; one statement per line; constants that matter to the reader (`NONEXISTENT_ID`) named at the top.
  Assertion literals list the fields proving the case, not the whole response. In API tests and support
  code: name the intermediate value (`const ordersWithoutItems = orders.filter(…)`, then the check); read the
  body, then pick from it — never `(await expectJson(…)).items.filter(…)[0]`; plain `filter`/`map`/loops,
  not `flatMap` tricks or `reduce` spreading objects; no tables of field-name strings (`row[count]`). Two or
  three variants: write the lines out; a table only for four or more, or one table driving several checks.
- Heavier code is justified for a table-driven `for` over variants of one rule, a Page Object method hiding a
  genuinely fiddly widget, or a short comment on a quirk a reader would otherwise "fix". In review each smell
  alone is *Minor*, *Major* when they make the spec unreadable.

## The spec owns the scenario

Action, then verification, visible in the test body. Reusable technical logic (parsing, comparison, polling,
request building) never lives as a local function in a `.spec.ts`:

```ts
// bad — the steps, actions and checks are hidden in a local function
async function runSearchScenario(catalogPage, query) { … }
// good — the scenario stays in the test
await test.step("Search for shoes", async () => { await catalogPage.search("shoes"); });
await test.step("Verify every result matches", async () => { expectAllNamed(await catalogPage.results.names(), "shoes"); });
```

The narrow exception: a helper used once in the same file, ≤ ~3 lines, hiding no action under test and no
parsing, sync or comparison. In doubt, it goes to its home.

## One home per kind

| Responsibility | Lives in |
|---|---|
| reusable checks, invariants, `shown<Block>` expectations, display helpers, response schemas | `src/assertions/<app\|shared>/` |
| locators, actions, readers | `src/pages/<app>/`, `src/components/<app>/` |
| what a page sends or receives, when a test checks it | the spec via `captureRequests` / `whenAnswered`; request patterns in `src/setup/<app>/` |
| preparing the browser before a page opens; stand lookups, multi-step preparation without teardown | `src/setup/<app>/` |
| payloads, form values, datasets, unique values | `src/data/builders\|datasets\|generators/` |
| an API area's requests and body types | `src/api/clients/<app>/` |
| what two or more tests stand on, disposable records, bound clients | `src/fixtures/<app>/data.fixture.ts` |
| logging (the only allowed console output) / redaction beyond `maskSecrets` | `src/logging/safe-logger.helper.ts` / `src/security/` |

No second helper for the same responsibility — extend the existing one. An assertion helper computes from
data the spec already has; one that fetches something is no assertion helper.

## The same thing twice → one helper in its home

Written in a **second** test, the same preparation or check becomes a named helper (a plain function, not a
fixture):

| The repeated piece | Its home | Example |
|---|---|---|
| a lookup or constant over one resource (an id by name) | that resource's client file | `searchOptions(criteria).category("Books")` |
| a default request body | the builder, called inside the client | `productSearch.search({ categoryIds: [id] })` |
| the response's body type | the client (`json<T>(…)`) | no `expectJson<T>(…)` in specs |
| reading, acting on or addressing a page (its URL and query format) | the Page Object | `productSearchPage.open({ categoryIds: [id] })` |
| how a block shows data, a rule its figures follow — even in one test | `src/assertions/<app>/` | `shownDelivery(order)`, `expectRowsAddUp(figures)` |
| a check used by 2+ specs | `src/assertions/<shared\|app>/` | `expectEveryItemMatches(…)` |

## No repeats

The same preparation, lookup or check in two places is one thing in its home — in specs, Page Objects and
setup alike. `qa:gate` fails two copied lines (within a file, between files of one app, out of shared code)
and the same `Setup:` step in two tests; lines that only look alike (a test's skeleton, look-alike checks, the
parts a Page Object composes) are not copies. The fix is the smallest clear abstraction in its home — never a
generic `utils` module, never a comment justifying the copy. Across apps, what no app owns has one copy in a
shared home every app imports. One manual case, one test (`specs.mdc` §Titles).
- **One way per operation**: once a helper exists every spec uses it — no hand-rolled variant beside it
  (`options.find((o) => o.name === name)!` next to `searchOptions(…)`).
- **No workaround for instability** in a spec: no fallback navigation, manual retry loops, broad `try/catch`
  swallowing a failure, or a comment explaining flakiness instead of fixing it.

## When an abstraction earns its cost

- Inline simple one-off logic. Abstract only when it improves readability, reuse, ownership, correctness,
  diagnostics or consistency — never speculatively — and never so that it hides the action under test, the
  main assertion, important behaviour, a setup risk or an open contract or product question.
- No helper for one trivial line, one obvious `expect`, one-off values or data, simple constants. A helper is
  justified when reused, non-trivial (parsing, normalizing, sorting, filtering, comparing), giving domain
  vocabulary, centralizing a contract-sensitive check or removing repeated technical noise; used once it is
  fine when it hides complex mechanics, suspicious when it wraps an obvious assertion.
- Assertion helpers may use `expect`; they never act, send requests, create data, hide the action, weaken a
  check or carry workflow logic, and are never named for action and check together (`sortAndVerify`,
  `submitAndValidate`, `createAndCheck`).
- Export only what is used outside its module. Ownership: a shared contract lives in a neutral shared file,
  not in one feature's (a user schema used by sign-up and sign-in); feature helpers stay with their feature;
  `shared/` only when genuinely shared. API and UI may check alike when each proves its own risk — never one
  helper hiding which layer means what. Where no shared home fits, two or three variants written out beat a
  clever generic utility.
- Specs hold small constants, local scenario cases, simple one-off checks, steps and calls to Page Objects
  and helpers — never reusable parsing, sorting or comparison algorithms, normalization, repeated predicates,
  response validation, generic helpers, builders or random data.
- **Cleanup triggers** (the verify skill's review; the fix is a refactor on request, `ui.mdc` §Ownership
  decisions and refactoring): an export nobody imports; a helper left after coverage shrank;
  a helper used once obscuring the scenario; a shared contract in a feature module; duplicated predicates;
  schema optionality contradicting an assertion; an assertion helper acting or preparing; a client, Page
  Object or component checking; a fixture hiding the action; a builder for one-off data; a component for a
  simple one-off block; methods for future tests. Recommend small batches — keep, remove, internalize, move,
  inline — never a mass refactor unless the architecture is clearly broken.

## Sorting and filtering

A fixed count or order on data the test does not control proves nothing. Prove the behaviour with the
template's `src/assertions/shared/sort-filter.ts` — never a comparison loop in a spec:
- `expectSorted(values, label, "asc" | "desc", { strict? })` — in order (equal neighbours allowed unless
  `strict`); a failure names the first pair out of order. `expectEveryItemMatches(items, { label, name,
  matches })` — at least one item and every one matches; a failure names the ones that do not. Both log what
  a passing check inspected through `safeLogger` — never a console summary of your own.
- Values are normalized first (prices and percentages as numbers, dates as timestamps, names lower-cased); in
  UI tests the reader returns text as shown and the app's display helper converts it
  (`src/assertions/<app>/display.ts`, `pricesOf(shown)`) — never `map` or `parseFloat` in the spec. A filter's predicate is wrapped once as a named
  rule in `src/assertions/<app>/` (`expectAllInStock(items)`), which the spec calls.
- Filtering: every visible item matches; a positive case has at least one result; a negative case asserts
  the empty state. Reset: controls and state back at baseline, not the exact original order. Paginated or
  virtualized lists: the loaded page only, unless the case is cross-page — never "the whole dataset is
  sorted" from one page. Exact counts or order only on controlled data (API-seeded, mocked) or when the count
  is under test. Sorting needs two distinct values on the stand, a positive filter a matching item —
  otherwise the precondition fails with `expectStandData`.

## Failures explain themselves

- Actionable: the action, expected vs observed, the endpoint, element or fixture involved — never a broad
  `try/catch` that logs and continues, never a generic message instead of the real failure. The spec
  contributes names only (`expect(x, "Add address button")`, `expectJson` / `expectStatus`: `/api/… answered
  400 instead of 201` + the body); missing stand data fails through `standDataMissing` / `expectStandData`.
- The template adds (`src/reporting/readable-error.ts`, the `page` fixture): the call log dropped, the
  check's name first, Playwright phrases as sentences, long values by shape, codes and tokens in URLs masked,
  `Page: <url>`, **Background errors** (server 5xx, page and console errors — also on passing tests),
  "Messages the app showed", a new tab opened, "The shared session ended during the run".
- `src/reporting/failure-cause.ts` opens each failure with its verdict and ends it with what to check by hand
  — a hint for the first look, never the answer; a bug is decided by a retest by hand:

| Verdict | Causes | First look |
|---|---|---|
| Known bug looks fixed — retest the ticket | a test of `specs/known-bugs.md` passed | the ticket, by hand |
| Possible bug — retest by hand | check did not match · element not found · server error | the step by hand against the case: as the run saw → a bug or a changed requirement; as the case expects → the test or its data; another element name → its Page Object |
| Not a bug — the stand or its data | stand data · stand unavailable (no answer, 502–504) · sign-in or access (401/403) · out of time · cleanup | the item, page or request named; one sign-in by hand, never a retried password; restore the data or change the lookup; rerun once the stand is up |
| Not a bug — the test needs fixing | test code (a TypeError in the suite's code) · element not found in a spec not committed yet (new or changed — its locator is not proven on the stand) | the test's own frame; the locator, live |
| Known bug — waiting for the fix | a known-bug test failing at its step | nothing |

- Three colours only: green passed, red failed, grey a known bug still failing at its step; a pass on the
  retry stays green, marked unstable. Allure's Categories tab lists the verdicts in that order, split by
  cause, plus "Unstable — passed on a retry".
- **The run's summary** — in the terminal, `reports/run-summary.md` (with what to check by hand) and
  `reports/run-summary.json` — lists possible bugs first and tests whose cleanup failed; healing starts there.
- Intentional output only through `safeLogger`; never secrets, tokens, cookies or full payloads.

## The Allure report

- `src/reporting/readable-allure.reporter.ts` (with `report-tree.ts`) fills every test — also one that failed
  before its body ran — from its path, tags and annotations: the tree `<App> › <Area> › <Feature> › API | UI ›
  test`, `<App> › User journeys (E2E) › <Area> › test`, `<App> › Smoke · is the stand alive › Sign-in` (in a
  smoke run the features' smoke tests appear under Smoke too); `layer` from the folder; the app as a
  `platform` label and tag (several apps → a report per app beside the all-apps one); `severity` from the
  annotation (major → Allure normal; without one `@smoke` → critical, `@regression` → normal).
  `allure-playwright` records the project, describe title and tags; failure screenshots, traces and videos
  attach to the failed test; tooling labels are dropped.
- **Specs, Page Objects, clients and data never call Allure** (`allure-js-commons`, `allure-playwright`,
  `allure.*`) — only `src/reporting/` does. Links come from annotations (`tms(<id>)`, a known bug's ticket
  from `specs/known-bugs.md`); extra text is `testInfo.attach(name, { body: maskSecrets(text), contentType:
  "text/plain" })`; `test.step` is the step structure, never Allure steps.
- Every npm test script clears the last results first (`npm run test:app -- <app> [layer] [targets]`); `npm run allure:open`
  generates and serves, `npm run allure:report` only writes `reports/allure/html`. A feature's API and UI in
  one report → one command (`npm run test:app -- <app> tests/api/<…> tests/ui/<…>`). Never pass `--reporter`
  on the command line — it replaces the config's reporters and Allure results silently stop. History:
  `reports/allure/history.jsonl` (git-ignored), trends need two generates. The report shows real stand data:
  publish it with restricted access.

## Flaky tests

A flaky test passes and fails on the same code and stand — a defect of the test, its data or the stand,
never of the assertion, and never hidden by waits, retries in the test body or a softened check.
- `playwright.config.ts` retries once locally, twice on CI, the E2E project once, smoke never; a pass on the
  retry is reported unstable. A test written or healed now passes without it: `--retries=0`, or
  `--repeat-each 5` locally. One timeout once is not yet "the stand was slow": `--repeat-each 3` first.
- Classify, fix only that: **timing / locator** ("not found / not visible / detached", passes on rerun) → the
  right condition, a stable locator in the Page Object · **data collision** (fails only in parallel or twice
  in a row, "already exists", a wrong count, someone else's record) → unique disposable data, cleanup that
  verifies, the same lock for a shared state; "found X, which tests change" → take its lock or leave it out
  of the lookup · **the stand** (whole runs fail, every request times out, 5xx) → no test change, a change-log
  note, rerun when it is up.
- **Quarantine** when the cause cannot be fixed now (it needs data or a team decision) or the test was flaky
  in 2 consecutive nightly runs: `@flaky` **instead of** `@regression` (a smoke test drops `@smoke` too) with
  `// flaky: <cause> — <ticket or plan note>` above it; it runs only in `npm run test:flaky` (nightly, allowed
  to fail). Exit: 5 consecutive green runs there (or `--repeat-each 5` after the fix) → back to
  `@regression`, the fix noted in the plan. Never a quarantine older than two sprints without a ticket.
  `@wip` works the same with `// wip: <reason>`. A test failing on a filed bug is not flaky
  (`plans.mdc` §Known bugs).

## Healing

Only when the plan still holds (`plans.mdc` §When the product changes). Evidence first: the command, the
error excerpt (no secrets), the spec, the run summary and the failure's verdict; a locator suspicion is
re-inspected live (`live-app.mdc`), never patched from a guess. **Allowed**: a locator in its Page Object, a
real readiness wait, a payload matching the documented contract, fixture wiring, idempotent cleanup.
**Forbidden**: `waitForTimeout`, a timeout or retries as the only change, softening a check, a `try/catch`
around a check, new coverage, a guessed status or text, tidying files unrelated to the failure. Done =
`npm run test:app -- <app> <layer> <spec> --retries=0` passes, then `npm run qa:gate`.
